WEBVTT

00:00:00.282 --> 00:00:01.336
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Part 2

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:09.408
<v><b>William:</b></v> I could picture the windmill I wanted to build,

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:16.197
but before I attempted something that big, I wanted to experiment with a small model first.

00:00:17.024 --> 00:00:19.226
I found an empty plastic jar… .

00:00:19.546 --> 00:00:26.535
Leaving the lid intact, I sawed off the bottom of the jar, then cut the sides into four large strips,

00:00:26.787 --> 00:00:29.016
then fanned them out into blades.

00:00:29.922 --> 00:00:34.910
I poked a hole through the centre of the lid and nailed it to one of the bamboo poles

00:00:34.936 --> 00:00:40.031
my father was saving.... I planted the pole in the ground behind the kitchen.

00:00:40.844 --> 00:00:49.174
But the wind hardly moved this contraption at all. The blades were too short. I needed extensions.

00:00:51.328 --> 00:00:58.855
The floors in our bathhouses often fill with water so we install PVC pipes to serve as a drain.

00:00:59.335 --> 00:01:05.712
Several years earlier, a bathhouse…collapsed, and they simply built another one beside it.

00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:10.225
I knew there was a piece of pipe still buried under the bricks,

00:01:10.534 --> 00:01:14.727
and after 20 minutes of digging around, I pulled it free.

00:01:15.601 --> 00:01:20.245
I sawed off a long section, then cut it down the middle from top to bottom.

00:01:21.132 --> 00:01:26.568
I stoked the fire in my mother’s kitchen, then held the pipe over the coals.

00:01:27.083 --> 00:01:34.963
Soon it began to warp and blacken, becoming soft and easy to bend, like wet banana leaves.

00:01:36.334 --> 00:01:42.807
Before the plastic could cool, I placed it on the ground and pressed it flat with a piece of iron sheet.

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:49.399
I then carved four blades with a saw, each one maybe 20 cm long.

00:01:50.888 --> 00:01:57.826
I didn’t have a drill, so I had to make my own. First I heated a long nail in the fire,

00:01:58.313 --> 00:02:02.330
then drove it through half a maize cob, creating a handle.

00:02:03.631 --> 00:02:07.519
I placed the nail back on the coals until it became red hot,

00:02:07.623 --> 00:02:14.064
then used it to bore holes into both sets of plastic blades. I then wired them together.

00:02:15.158 --> 00:02:22.491
I didn’t have any pliers, so I used two bicycle spokes to bend and tighten the wires on the blades.

00:02:24.052 --> 00:02:26.001
That’s when my mother arrived.

00:02:27.061 --> 00:02:32.973
‘What are you doing messing in my kitchen?’ she said. ‘Get these toys out of here.’

00:02:33.551 --> 00:02:37.727
I tried to explain about windmills and my plan to generate power,

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.235
but all she saw were some pieces of plastic stuck to a bamboo stick.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:49.071
‘Go help your father in the fields,’ she said. …It was pointless to explain.

00:02:50.106 --> 00:02:55.107
What I needed now was a bicycle dynamo or some kind of generator,

00:02:55.619 --> 00:02:59.129
and I had no idea where I was going to find such a thing.